(function(){if(!window.__hermes_register_book)return;window.__hermes_register_book("alc1-b",[{"sp":"HDR","t":"ALCIBIADES I"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I dare say that you may be surprised to find, O son of Cleinias, that I, who am your first lover, not having spoken to you for many years, when the rest of the world were wearying you with their attentions, am the last of your lovers who still speaks to you. The cause of my silence has been that I was hindered by a power more than human, of which I will some day explain to you the nature; this impediment has now been removed; I therefore here present myself before you, and I greatly hope that no similar hindrance will again occur. Meanwhile, I have observed that your pride has been too much for the pride of your admirers; they were numerous and high-spirited, but they have all run away, overpowered by your superior force of character; not one of them remains. And I want you to understand the reason why you have been too much for them. You think that you have no need of them or of any other man, for you have great possessions and lack nothing, beginning with the body, and ending with the soul. In the first place, you say to yourself that you are the fairest and tallest of the citizens, and this every one who has eyes may see to be true; in the second place, that you are among the noblest of them, highly connected both on the father’s and the mother’s side, and sprung from one of the most distinguished families in your own state, which is the greatest in Hellas, and having many friends and kinsmen of the best sort, who can assist you when in need; and there is one potent relative, who is more to you than all the rest, Pericles the son of Xanthippus, whom your father left guardian of you, and of your brother, and who can do as he pleases not only in this city, but in all Hellas, and among many and mighty barbarous nations. Moreover, you are rich; but I must say that you value yourself least of all upon your possessions. And all these things have lifted you up; you have overcome your lovers, and they have acknowledged that you were too much for them. Have you not remarked their absence? And now I know that you wonder why I, unlike the rest of them, have not gone away, and what can be my motive in remaining.","st":"103a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Perhaps, Socrates, you are not aware that I was just going to ask you the very same question — What do you want? And what is your motive in annoying me, and always, wherever I am, making a point of coming? (Compare Symp.) I do really wonder what you mean, and should greatly like to know.","st":"103a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then if, as you say, you desire to know, I suppose that you will be willing to hear, and I may consider myself to be speaking to an auditor who will remain, and will not run away?","st":"104d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly, let me hear.","st":"104d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> You had better be careful, for I may very likely be as unwilling to end as I have hitherto been to begin.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Proceed, my good man, and I will listen.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I will proceed; and, although no lover likes to speak with one who has no feeling of love in him (compare Symp.), I will make an effort, and tell you what I meant: My love, Alcibiades, which I hardly like to confess, would long ago have passed away, as I flatter myself, if I saw you loving your good things, or thinking that you ought to pass life in the enjoyment of them. But I shall reveal other thoughts of yours, which you keep to yourself; whereby you will know that I have always had my eye on you. Suppose that at this moment some God came to you and said: Alcibiades, will you live as you are, or die in an instant if you are forbidden to make any further acquisition? — I verily believe that you would choose death. And I will tell you the hope in which you are at present living: Before many days have elapsed, you think that you will come before the Athenian assembly, and will prove to them that you are more worthy of honour than Pericles, or any other man that ever lived, and having proved this, you will have the greatest power in the state. When you have gained the greatest power among us, you will go on to other Hellenic states, and not only to Hellenes, but to all the barbarians who inhabit the same continent with us. And if the God were then to say to you again: Here in Europe is to be your seat of empire, and you must not cross over into Asia or meddle with Asiatic affairs, I do not believe that you would choose to live upon these terms; but the world, as I may say, must be filled with your power and name — no man less than Cyrus and Xerxes is of any account with you. Such I know to be your hopes — I am not guessing only — and very likely you, who know that I am speaking the truth, will reply, Well, Socrates, but what have my hopes to do with the explanation which you promised of your unwillingness to leave me? And that is what I am now going to tell you, sweet son of Cleinias and Dinomache. The explanation is, that all these designs of yours cannot be accomplished by you without my help; so great is the power which I believe myself to have over you and your concerns; and this I conceive to be the reason why the God has hitherto forbidden me to converse with you, and I have been long expecting his permission. For, as you hope to prove your own great value to the state, and having proved it, to attain at once to absolute power, so do I indulge a hope that I shall be the supreme power over you, if I am able to prove my own great value to you, and to show you that neither guardian, nor kinsman, nor any one is able to deliver into your hands the power which you desire, but I only, God being my helper. When you were young (compare Symp.) and your hopes were not yet matured, I should have wasted my time, and therefore, as I conceive, the God forbade me to converse with you; but now, having his permission, I will speak, for now you will listen to me.","st":"105a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Your silence, Socrates, was always a surprise to me. I never could understand why you followed me about, and now that you have begun to speak again, I am still more amazed. Whether I think all this or not, is a matter about which you seem to have already made up your mind, and therefore my denial will have no effect upon you. But granting, if I must, that you have perfectly divined my purposes, why is your assistance necessary to the attainment of them? Can you tell me why?","st":"105a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> You want to know whether I can make a long speech, such as you are in the habit of hearing; but that is not my way. I think, however, that I can prove to you the truth of what I am saying, if you will grant me one little favour.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes, if the favour which you mean be not a troublesome one.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Will you be troubled at having questions to answer?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Not at all.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then please to answer.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Ask me.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Have you not the intention which I attribute to you?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I will grant anything you like, in the hope of hearing what more you have to say.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> You do, then, mean, as I was saying, to come forward in a little while in the character of an adviser of the Athenians? And suppose that when you are ascending the bema, I pull you by the sleeve and say, Alcibiades, you are getting up to advise the Athenians — do you know the matter about which they are going to deliberate, better than they? — How would you answer?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I should reply, that I was going to advise them about a matter which I do know better than they.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then you are a good adviser about the things which you know?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"106d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And do you know anything but what you have learned of others, or found out yourself?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is all.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And would you have ever learned or discovered anything, if you had not been willing either to learn of others or to examine yourself?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I should not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And would you have been willing to learn or to examine what you supposed that you knew?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then there was a time when you thought that you did not know what you are now supposed to know?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"106e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I think that I know tolerably well the extent of your acquirements; and you must tell me if I forget any of them: according to my recollection, you learned the arts of writing, of playing on the lyre, and of wrestling; the flute you never would learn; this is the sum of your accomplishments, unless there were some which you acquired in secret; and I think that secrecy was hardly possible, as you could not have come out of your door, either by day or night, without my seeing you.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes, that was the whole of my schooling.","st":"107a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And are you going to get up in the Athenian assembly, and give them advice about writing?","st":"107a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No, indeed.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Or about the touch of the lyre?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And they are not in the habit of deliberating about wrestling, in the assembly?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Hardly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then what are the deliberations in which you propose to advise them? Surely not about building?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> For the builder will advise better than you will about that?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> He will.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Nor about divination?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> About that again the diviner will advise better than you will?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Whether he be little or great, good or ill-looking, noble or ignoble — makes no difference.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> A man is a good adviser about anything, not because he has riches, but because he has knowledge?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Assuredly.","st":"107c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Whether their counsellor is rich or poor, is not a matter which will make any difference to the Athenians when they are deliberating about the health of the citizens; they only require that he should be a physician.","st":"107c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Of course.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then what will be the subject of deliberation about which you will be justified in getting up and advising them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> About their own concerns, Socrates.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> You mean about shipbuilding, for example, when the question is what sort of ships they ought to build?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No, I should not advise them about that.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I suppose, because you do not understand shipbuilding: — is that the reason?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> It is.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then about what concerns of theirs will you advise them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> About war, Socrates, or about peace, or about any other concerns of the state.","st":"107d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> You mean, when they deliberate with whom they ought to make peace, and with whom they ought to go to war, and in what manner?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And they ought to go to war with those against whom it is better to go to war?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"107e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And when it is better?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And for as long a time as is better?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But suppose the Athenians to deliberate with whom they ought to close in wrestling, and whom they should grasp by the hand, would you, or the master of gymnastics, be a better adviser of them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly, the master of gymnastics.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And can you tell me on what grounds the master of gymnastics would decide, with whom they ought or ought not to close, and when and how? To take an instance: Would he not say that they should wrestle with those against whom it is best to wrestle?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And as much as is best?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And at such times as are best?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Again; you sometimes accompany the lyre with the song and dance?","st":"108b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"108b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> When it is well to do so?","st":"108b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"108b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And as much as is well?","st":"108b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Just so.","st":"108b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And as you speak of an excellence or art of the best in wrestling, and of an excellence in playing the lyre, I wish you would tell me what this latter is; — the excellence of wrestling I call gymnastic, and I want to know what you call the other.","st":"108b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I do not understand you.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then try to do as I do; for the answer which I gave is universally right, and when I say right, I mean according to rule.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"108c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And was not the art of which I spoke gymnastic?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And I called the excellence in wrestling gymnastic?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> You did.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And I was right?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I think that you were.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Well, now, — for you should learn to argue prettily — let me ask you in return to tell me, first, what is that art of which playing and singing, and stepping properly in the dance, are parts, — what is the name of the whole? I think that by this time you must be able to tell.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Indeed I cannot.","st":"108d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then let me put the matter in another way: what do you call the Goddesses who are the patronesses of art?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> The Muses do you mean, Socrates?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Yes, I do; and what is the name of the art which is called after them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I suppose that you mean music.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Yes, that is my meaning; and what is the excellence of the art of music, as I told you truly that the excellence of wrestling was gymnastic — what is the excellence of music — to be what?","st":"108e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> To be musical, I suppose.","st":"108e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Very good; and now please to tell me what is the excellence of war and peace; as the more musical was the more excellent, or the more gymnastical was the more excellent, tell me, what name do you give to the more excellent in war and peace?","st":"108e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> But I really cannot tell you.","st":"109a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But if you were offering advice to another and said to him — This food is better than that, at this time and in this quantity, and he said to you — What do you mean, Alcibiades, by the word ‘better’? you would have no difficulty in replying that you meant ‘more wholesome,’ although you do not profess to be a physician: and when the subject is one of which you profess to have knowledge, and about which you are ready to get up and advise as if you knew, are you not ashamed, when you are asked, not to be able to answer the question? Is it not disgraceful?","st":"109a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Well, then, consider and try to explain what is the meaning of ‘better,’ in the matter of making peace and going to war with those against whom you ought to go to war? To what does the word refer?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I am thinking, and I cannot tell.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But you surely know what are the charges which we bring against one another, when we arrive at the point of making war, and what name we give them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes, certainly; we say that deceit or violence has been employed, or that we have been defrauded.","st":"109b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And how does this happen? Will you tell me how? For there may be a difference in the manner.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Do you mean by ‘how,’ Socrates, whether we suffered these things justly or unjustly?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Exactly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> There can be no greater difference than between just and unjust.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And would you advise the Athenians to go to war with the just or with the unjust?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is an awkward question; for certainly, even if a person did intend to go to war with the just, he would not admit that they were just.","st":"109c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> He would not go to war, because it would be unlawful?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Neither lawful nor honourable.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then you, too, would address them on principles of justice?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> What, then, is justice but that better, of which I spoke, in going to war or not going to war with those against whom we ought or ought not, and when we ought or ought not to go to war?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But how is this, friend Alcibiades? Have you forgotten that you do not know this, or have you been to the schoolmaster without my knowledge, and has he taught you to discern the just from the unjust? Who is he? I wish you would tell me, that I may go and learn of him — you shall introduce me.","st":"109d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> You are mocking, Socrates.","st":"109d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> No, indeed; I most solemnly declare to you by Zeus, who is the God of our common friendship, and whom I never will forswear, that I am not; tell me, then, who this instructor is, if he exists.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> But, perhaps, he does not exist; may I not have acquired the knowledge of just and unjust in some other way?","st":"109e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Yes; if you have discovered them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> But do you not think that I could discover them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I am sure that you might, if you enquired about them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> And do you not think that I would enquire?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Yes; if you thought that you did not know them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> And was there not a time when I did so think?","st":"110a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Very good; and can you tell me how long it is since you thought that you did not know the nature of the just and the unjust? What do you say to a year ago? Were you then in a state of conscious ignorance and enquiry? Or did you think that you knew? And please to answer truly, that our discussion may not be in vain.","st":"110a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Well, I thought that I knew.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And two years ago, and three years ago, and four years ago, you knew all the same?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I did.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And more than four years ago you were a child — were you not?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"110b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And then I am quite sure that you thought you knew.","st":"110b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Why are you so sure?","st":"110c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Because I often heard you when a child, in your teacher’s house, or elsewhere, playing at dice or some other game with the boys, not hesitating at all about the nature of the just and unjust; but very confident — crying and shouting that one of the boys was a rogue and a cheat, and had been cheating. Is it not true?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> But what was I to do, Socrates, when anybody cheated me?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And how can you say, ‘What was I to do’? if at the time you did not know whether you were wronged or not?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> To be sure I knew; I was quite aware that I was being cheated.","st":"110d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then you suppose yourself even when a child to have known the nature of just and unjust?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly; and I did know then.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And when did you discover them — not, surely, at the time when you thought that you knew them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And when did you think that you were ignorant — if you consider, you will find that there never was such a time?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Really, Socrates, I cannot say.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then you did not learn them by discovering them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But just before you said that you did not know them by learning; now, if you have neither discovered nor learned them, how and whence do you come to know them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I suppose that I was mistaken in saying that I knew them through my own discovery of them; whereas, in truth, I learned them in the same way that other people learn.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> So you said before, and I must again ask, of whom? Do tell me.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Of the many.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Do you take refuge in them? I cannot say much for your teachers.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Why, are they not able to teach?","st":"111a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> They could not teach you how to play at draughts, which you would acknowledge (would you not) to be a much smaller matter than justice?","st":"111a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"111a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And can they teach the better who are unable to teach the worse?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I think that they can; at any rate, they can teach many far better things than to play at draughts.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> What things?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Why, for example, I learned to speak Greek of them, and I cannot say who was my teacher, or to whom I am to attribute my knowledge of Greek, if not to those good-for-nothing teachers, as you call them.","st":"111b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Why, yes, my friend; and the many are good enough teachers of Greek, and some of their instructions in that line may be justly praised.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Why is that?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Why, because they have the qualities which good teachers ought to have.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> What qualities?","st":"111c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Why, you know that knowledge is the first qualification of any teacher?","st":"111c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"111c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And if they know, they must agree together and not differ?","st":"111c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"111c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And would you say that they knew the things about which they differ?","st":"111c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No.","st":"111c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then how can they teach them?","st":"111c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> They cannot.","st":"111c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Well, but do you imagine that the many would differ about the nature of wood and stone? are they not agreed if you ask them what they are? and do they not run to fetch the same thing, when they want a piece of wood or a stone? And so in similar cases, which I suspect to be pretty nearly all that you mean by speaking Greek.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> These, as we were saying, are matters about which they are agreed with one another and with themselves; both individuals and states use the same words about them; they do not use some one word and some another.","st":"111d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> They do not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then they may be expected to be good teachers of these things?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And if we want to instruct any one in them, we shall be right in sending him to be taught by our friends the many?","st":"111e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very true.","st":"111e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But if we wanted further to know not only which are men and which are horses, but which men or horses have powers of running, would the many still be able to inform us?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And you have a sufficient proof that they do not know these things and are not the best teachers of them, inasmuch as they are never agreed about them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And suppose that we wanted to know not only what men are like, but what healthy or diseased men are like — would the many be able to teach us?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> They would not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And you would have a proof that they were bad teachers of these matters, if you saw them at variance?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I should.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Well, but are the many agreed with themselves, or with one another, about the justice or injustice of men and things?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Assuredly not, Socrates.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> There is no subject about which they are more at variance?","st":"112b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> None.","st":"112b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I do not suppose that you ever saw or heard of men quarrelling over the principles of health and disease to such an extent as to go to war and kill one another for the sake of them?","st":"112b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No indeed.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But of the quarrels about justice and injustice, even if you have never seen them, you have certainly heard from many people, including Homer; for you have heard of the Iliad and Odyssey?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> To be sure, Socrates.","st":"112c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> A difference of just and unjust is the argument of those poems?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Which difference caused all the wars and deaths of Trojans and Achaeans, and the deaths of the suitors of Penelope in their quarrel with Odysseus.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And when the Athenians and Lacedaemonians and Boeotians fell at Tanagra, and afterwards in the battle of Coronea, at which your father Cleinias met his end, the question was one of justice — this was the sole cause of the battles, and of their deaths.","st":"112d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But can they be said to understand that about which they are quarrelling to the death?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And yet those whom you thus allow to be ignorant are the teachers to whom you are appealing.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But how are you ever likely to know the nature of justice and injustice, about which you are so perplexed, if you have neither learned them of others nor discovered them yourself?","st":"112e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> From what you say, I suppose not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> See, again, how inaccurately you speak, Alcibiades!","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> In what respect?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> In saying that I say so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Why, did you not say that I know nothing of the just and unjust?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> No; I did not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Did I, then?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> How was that?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Let me explain. Suppose I were to ask you which is the greater number, two or one; you would reply ‘two’?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I should.","st":"113a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And by how much greater?","st":"113a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> By one.","st":"113a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Which of us now says that two is more than one?","st":"113a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I do.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Did not I ask, and you answer the question?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then who is speaking? I who put the question, or you who answer me?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I am.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Or suppose that I ask and you tell me the letters which make up the name Socrates, which of us is the speaker?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I am.","st":"113b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Now let us put the case generally: whenever there is a question and answer, who is the speaker, — the questioner or the answerer?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I should say, Socrates, that the answerer was the speaker.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And have I not been the questioner all through?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And you the answerer?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Just so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Which of us, then, was the speaker?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> The inference is, Socrates, that I was the speaker.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Did not some one say that Alcibiades, the fair son of Cleinias, not understanding about just and unjust, but thinking that he did understand, was going to the assembly to advise the Athenians about what he did not know? Was not that said?","st":"113c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very true.","st":"113c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then, Alcibiades, the result may be expressed in the language of Euripides. I think that you have heard all this ‘from yourself, and not from me’; nor did I say this, which you erroneously attribute to me, but you yourself, and what you said was very true. For indeed, my dear fellow, the design which you meditate of teaching what you do not know, and have not taken any pains to learn, is downright insanity.","st":"113d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> But, Socrates, I think that the Athenians and the rest of the Hellenes do not often advise as to the more just or unjust; for they see no difficulty in them, and therefore they leave them, and consider which course of action will be most expedient; for there is a difference between justice and expediency. Many persons have done great wrong and profited by their injustice; others have done rightly and come to no good.","st":"113d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Well, but granting that the just and the expedient are ever so much opposed, you surely do not imagine that you know what is expedient for mankind, or why a thing is expedient?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Why not, Socrates? — But I am not going to be asked again from whom I learned, or when I made the discovery.","st":"114a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> What a way you have! When you make a mistake which might be refuted by a previous argument, you insist on having a new and different refutation; the old argument is a worn-our garment which you will no longer put on, but some one must produce another which is clean and new. Now I shall disregard this move of yours, and shall ask over again, — Where did you learn and how do you know the nature of the expedient, and who is your teacher? All this I comprehend in a single question, and now you will manifestly be in the old difficulty, and will not be able to show that you know the expedient, either because you learned or because you discovered it yourself. But, as I perceive that you are dainty, and dislike the taste of a stale argument, I will enquire no further into your knowledge of what is expedient or what is not expedient for the Athenian people, and simply request you to say why you do not explain whether justice and expediency are the same or different? And if you like you may examine me as I have examined you, or, if you would rather, you may carry on the discussion by yourself.","st":"114a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> But I am not certain, Socrates, whether I shall be able to discuss the matter with you.","st":"114a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then imagine, my dear fellow, that I am the demus and the ecclesia; for in the ecclesia, too, you will have to persuade men individually.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And is not the same person able to persuade one individual singly and many individuals of the things which he knows? The grammarian, for example, can persuade one and he can persuade many about letters.","st":"114c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"114c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And about number, will not the same person persuade one and persuade many?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And this will be he who knows number, or the arithmetician?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And cannot you persuade one man about that of which you can persuade many?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I suppose so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And that of which you can persuade either is clearly what you know?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"114d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And the only difference between one who argues as we are doing, and the orator who is addressing an assembly, is that the one seeks to persuade a number, and the other an individual, of the same things.","st":"114d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I suppose so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Well, then, since the same person who can persuade a multitude can persuade individuals, try conclusions upon me, and prove to me that the just is not always expedient.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> You take liberties, Socrates.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I shall take the liberty of proving to you the opposite of that which you will not prove to me.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Proceed.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Answer my questions — that is all.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Nay, I should like you to be the speaker.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> What, do you not wish to be persuaded?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly I do.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And can you be persuaded better than out of your own mouth?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I think not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then you shall answer; and if you do not hear the words, that the just is the expedient, coming from your own lips, never believe another man again.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I won’t; but answer I will, for I do not see how I can come to any harm.","st":"115a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> A true prophecy! Let me begin then by enquiring of you whether you allow that the just is sometimes expedient and sometimes not?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And sometimes honourable and sometimes not?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> What do you mean?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I am asking if you ever knew any one who did what was dishonourable and yet just?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Never.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> All just things are honourable?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And are honourable things sometimes good and sometimes not good, or are they always good?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I rather think, Socrates, that some honourable things are evil.","st":"115b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And are some dishonourable things good?","st":"115b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"115b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> You mean in such a case as the following: — In time of war, men have been wounded or have died in rescuing a companion or kinsman, when others who have neglected the duty of rescuing them have escaped in safety?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And to rescue another under such circumstances is honourable, in respect of the attempt to save those whom we ought to save; and this is courage?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"115c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But evil in respect of death and wounds?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And the courage which is shown in the rescue is one thing, and the death another?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then the rescue of one’s friends is honourable in one point of view, but evil in another?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And if honourable, then also good: Will you consider now whether I may not be right, for you were acknowledging that the courage which is shown in the rescue is honourable? Now is this courage good or evil? Look at the matter thus: which would you rather choose, good or evil?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Good.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And the greatest goods you would be most ready to choose, and would least like to be deprived of them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> What would you say of courage? At what price would you be willing to be deprived of courage?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I would rather die than be a coward.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then you think that cowardice is the worst of evils?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I do.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> As bad as death, I suppose?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And life and courage are the extreme opposites of death and cowardice?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And they are what you would most desire to have, and their opposites you would least desire?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Is this because you think life and courage the best, and death and cowardice the worst?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And you would term the rescue of a friend in battle honourable, in as much as courage does a good work?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I should.","st":"116a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But evil because of the death which ensues?","st":"116a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"116a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Might we not describe their different effects as follows: — You may call either of them evil in respect of the evil which is the result, and good in respect of the good which is the result of either of them?","st":"116a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And they are honourable in so far as they are good, and dishonourable in so far as they are evil?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then when you say that the rescue of a friend in battle is honourable and yet evil, that is equivalent to saying that the rescue is good and yet evil?","st":"116b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I believe that you are right, Socrates.","st":"116b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Nothing honourable, regarded as honourable, is evil; nor anything base, regarded as base, good.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Look at the matter yet once more in a further light: he who acts honourably acts well?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And he who acts well is happy?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Of course.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And the happy are those who obtain good?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And they obtain good by acting well and honourably?","st":"116c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"116c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then acting well is a good?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And happiness is a good?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then the good and the honourable are again identified.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Manifestly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then, if the argument holds, what we find to be honourable we shall also find to be good?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And is the good expedient or not?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Expedient.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Do you remember our admissions about the just?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes; if I am not mistaken, we said that those who acted justly must also act honourably.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And the honourable is the good?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And the good is expedient?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then, Alcibiades, the just is expedient?","st":"116e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I should infer so.","st":"116e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And all this I prove out of your own mouth, for I ask and you answer?","st":"116e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I must acknowledge it to be true.","st":"116e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And having acknowledged that the just is the same as the expedient, are you not (let me ask) prepared to ridicule any one who, pretending to understand the principles of justice and injustice, gets up to advise the noble Athenians or the ignoble Peparethians, that the just may be the evil?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I solemnly declare, Socrates, that I do not know what I am saying. Verily, I am in a strange state, for when you put questions to me I am of different minds in successive instants.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And are you not aware of the nature of this perplexity, my friend?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Indeed I am not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Do you suppose that if some one were to ask you whether you have two eyes or three, or two hands or four, or anything of that sort, you would then be of different minds in successive instants?","st":"117a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I begin to distrust myself, but still I do not suppose that I should.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> You would feel no doubt; and for this reason — because you would know?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I suppose so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And the reason why you involuntarily contradict yourself is clearly that you are ignorant?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very likely.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And if you are perplexed in answering about just and unjust, honourable and dishonourable, good and evil, expedient and inexpedient, the reason is that you are ignorant of them, and therefore in perplexity. Is not that clear?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I agree.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But is this always the case, and is a man necessarily perplexed about that of which he has no knowledge?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly he is.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And do you know how to ascend into heaven?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And in this case, too, is your judgment perplexed?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Do you see the reason why, or shall I tell you?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Tell me.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> The reason is, that you not only do not know, my friend, but you do not think that you know.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> There again; what do you mean?","st":"117c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Ask yourself; are you in any perplexity about things of which you are ignorant? You know, for example, that you know nothing about the preparation of food.","st":"117c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And do you think and perplex yourself about the preparation of food: or do you leave that to some one who understands the art?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> The latter.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Or if you were on a voyage, would you bewilder yourself by considering whether the rudder is to be drawn inwards or outwards, or do you leave that to the pilot, and do nothing?","st":"117d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> It would be the concern of the pilot.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then you are not perplexed about what you do not know, if you know that you do not know it?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I imagine not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Do you not see, then, that mistakes in life and practice are likewise to be attributed to the ignorance which has conceit of knowledge?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Once more, what do you mean?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I suppose that we begin to act when we think that we know what we are doing?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"117e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But when people think that they do not know, they entrust their business to others?","st":"117e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And so there is a class of ignorant persons who do not make mistakes in life, because they trust others about things of which they are ignorant?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Who, then, are the persons who make mistakes? They cannot, of course, be those who know?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"118a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But if neither those who know, nor those who know that they do not know, make mistakes, there remain those only who do not know and think that they know.","st":"118a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes, only those.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then this is ignorance of the disgraceful sort which is mischievous?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And most mischievous and most disgraceful when having to do with the greatest matters?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> By far.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And can there be any matters greater than the just, the honourable, the good, and the expedient?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> There cannot be.","st":"118b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And these, as you were saying, are what perplex you?","st":"118b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"118b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But if you are perplexed, then, as the previous argument has shown, you are not only ignorant of the greatest matters, but being ignorant you fancy that you know them?","st":"118b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I fear that you are right.","st":"118c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And now see what has happened to you, Alcibiades! I hardly like to speak of your evil case, but as we are alone I will: My good friend, you are wedded to ignorance of the most disgraceful kind, and of this you are convicted, not by me, but out of your own mouth and by your own argument; wherefore also you rush into politics before you are educated. Neither is your case to be deemed singular. For I might say the same of almost all our statesmen, with the exception, perhaps of your guardian, Pericles.","st":"118c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes, Socrates; and Pericles is said not to have got his wisdom by the light of nature, but to have associated with several of the philosophers; with Pythocleides, for example, and with Anaxagoras, and now in advanced life with Damon, in the hope of gaining wisdom.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Very good; but did you ever know a man wise in anything who was unable to impart his particular wisdom? For example, he who taught you letters was not only wise, but he made you and any others whom he liked wise.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"118d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And you, whom he taught, can do the same?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And in like manner the harper and gymnastic-master?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> When a person is enabled to impart knowledge to another, he thereby gives an excellent proof of his own understanding of any matter.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I agree.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Well, and did Pericles make any one wise; did he begin by making his sons wise?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> But, Socrates, if the two sons of Pericles were simpletons, what has that to do with the matter?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Well, but did he make your brother, Cleinias, wise?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Cleinias is a madman; there is no use in talking of him.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But if Cleinias is a madman and the two sons of Pericles were simpletons, what reason can be given why he neglects you, and lets you be as you are?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I believe that I am to blame for not listening to him.","st":"119a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But did you ever hear of any other Athenian or foreigner, bond or free, who was deemed to have grown wiser in the society of Pericles, — as I might cite Pythodorus, the son of Isolochus, and Callias, the son of Calliades, who have grown wiser in the society of Zeno, for which privilege they have each of them paid him the sum of a hundred minae (about 406 pounds sterling) to the increase of their wisdom and fame.","st":"119a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I certainly never did hear of any one.","st":"119b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Well, and in reference to your own case, do you mean to remain as you are, or will you take some pains about yourself?","st":"119b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> With your aid, Socrates, I will. And indeed, when I hear you speak, the truth of what you are saying strikes home to me, and I agree with you, for our statesmen, all but a few, do appear to be quite uneducated.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> What is the inference?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Why, that if they were educated they would be trained athletes, and he who means to rival them ought to have knowledge and experience when he attacks them; but now, as they have become politicians without any special training, why should I have the trouble of learning and practising? For I know well that by the light of nature I shall get the better of them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> My dear friend, what a sentiment! And how unworthy of your noble form and your high estate!","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> What do you mean, Socrates; why do you say so?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I am grieved when I think of our mutual love.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> At what?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> At your fancying that the contest on which you are entering is with people here.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Why, what others are there?","st":"119d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Is that a question which a magnanimous soul should ask?","st":"119d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Do you mean to say that the contest is not with these?","st":"119e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And suppose that you were going to steer a ship into action, would you only aim at being the best pilot on board? Would you not, while acknowledging that you must possess this degree of excellence, rather look to your antagonists, and not, as you are now doing, to your fellow combatants? You ought to be so far above these latter, that they will not even dare to be your rivals; and, being regarded by you as inferiors, will do battle for you against the enemy; this is the kind of superiority which you must establish over them, if you mean to accomplish any noble action really worthy of yourself and of the state.","st":"119e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That would certainly be my aim.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Verily, then, you have good reason to be satisfied, if you are better than the soldiers; and you need not, when you are their superior and have your thoughts and actions fixed upon them, look away to the generals of the enemy.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Of whom are you speaking, Socrates?","st":"120a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Why, you surely know that our city goes to war now and then with the Lacedaemonians and with the great king?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True enough.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And if you meant to be the ruler of this city, would you not be right in considering that the Lacedaemonian and Persian king were your true rivals?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I believe that you are right.","st":"120b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Oh no, my friend, I am quite wrong, and I think that you ought rather to turn your attention to Midias the quail-breeder and others like him, who manage our politics; in whom, as the women would remark, you may still see the slaves’ cut of hair, cropping out in their minds as well as on their pates; and they come with their barbarous lingo to flatter us and not to rule us. To these, I say, you should look, and then you need not trouble yourself about your own fitness to contend in such a noble arena: there is no reason why you should either learn what has to be learned, or practise what has to be practised, and only when thoroughly prepared enter on a political career.","st":"120b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> There, I think, Socrates, that you are right; I do not suppose, however, that the Spartan generals or the great king are really different from anybody else.","st":"120c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But, my dear friend, do consider what you are saying.","st":"120d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> What am I to consider?","st":"120d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> In the first place, will you be more likely to take care of yourself, if you are in a wholesome fear and dread of them, or if you are not?","st":"120e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly, if I have such a fear of them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And do you think that you will sustain any injury if you take care of yourself?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No, I shall be greatly benefited.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And this is one very important respect in which that notion of yours is bad.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> In the next place, consider that what you say is probably false.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> How so?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Let me ask you whether better natures are likely to be found in noble races or not in noble races?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly in noble races.","st":"121a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Are not those who are well born and well bred most likely to be perfect in virtue?","st":"121b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"121b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then let us compare our antecedents with those of the Lacedaemonian and Persian kings; are they inferior to us in descent? Have we not heard that the former are sprung from Heracles, and the latter from Achaemenes, and that the race of Heracles and the race of Achaemenes go back to Perseus, son of Zeus?","st":"121b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Why, so does mine go back to Eurysaces, and he to Zeus!","st":"121b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And mine, noble Alcibiades, to Daedalus, and he to Hephaestus, son of Zeus. But, for all that, we are far inferior to them. For they are descended ‘from Zeus,’ through a line of kings — either kings of Argos and Lacedaemon, or kings of Persia, a country which the descendants of Achaemenes have always possessed, besides being at various times sovereigns of Asia, as they now are; whereas, we and our fathers were but private persons. How ridiculous would you be thought if you were to make a display of your ancestors and of Salamis the island of Eurysaces, or of Aegina, the habitation of the still more ancient Aeacus, before Artaxerxes, son of Xerxes. You should consider how inferior we are to them both in the derivation of our birth and in other particulars. Did you never observe how great is the property of the Spartan kings? And their wives are under the guardianship of the Ephori, who are public officers and watch over them, in order to preserve as far as possible the purity of the Heracleid blood. Still greater is the difference among the Persians; for no one entertains a suspicion that the father of a prince of Persia can be any one but the king. Such is the awe which invests the person of the queen, that any other guard is needless. And when the heir of the kingdom is born, all the subjects of the king feast; and the day of his birth is for ever afterwards kept as a holiday and time of sacrifice by all Asia; whereas, when you and I were born, Alcibiades, as the comic poet says, the neighbours hardly knew of the important event. After the birth of the royal child, he is tended, not by a good-for-nothing woman-nurse, but by the best of the royal eunuchs, who are charged with the care of him, and especially with the fashioning and right formation of his limbs, in order that he may be as shapely as possible; which being their calling, they are held in great honour. And when the young prince is seven years old he is put upon a horse and taken to the riding-masters, and begins to go out hunting. And at fourteen years of age he is handed over to the royal schoolmasters, as they are termed: these are four chosen men, reputed to be the best among the Persians of a certain age; and one of them is the wisest, another the justest, a third the most temperate, and a fourth the most valiant. The first instructs him in the magianism of Zoroaster, the son of Oromasus, which is the worship of the Gods, and teaches him also the duties of his royal office; the second, who is the justest, teaches him always to speak the truth; the third, or most temperate, forbids him to allow any pleasure to be lord over him, that he may be accustomed to be a freeman and king indeed, — lord of himself first, and not a slave; the most valiant trains him to be bold and fearless, telling him that if he fears he is to deem himself a slave; whereas Pericles gave you, Alcibiades, for a tutor Zopyrus the Thracian, a slave of his who was past all other work. I might enlarge on the nurture and education of your rivals, but that would be tedious; and what I have said is a sufficient sample of what remains to be said. I have only to remark, by way of contrast, that no one cares about your birth or nurture or education, or, I may say, about that of any other Athenian, unless he has a lover who looks after him. And if you cast an eye on the wealth, the luxury, the garments with their flowing trains, the anointings with myrrh, the multitudes of attendants, and all the other bravery of the Persians, you will be ashamed when you discern your own inferiority; or if you look at the temperance and orderliness and ease and grace and magnanimity and courage and endurance and love of toil and desire of glory and ambition of the Lacedaemonians — in all these respects you will see that you are but a child in comparison of them. Even in the matter of wealth, if you value yourself upon that, I must reveal to you how you stand; for if you form an estimate of the wealth of the Lacedaemonians, you will see that our possessions fall far short of theirs. For no one here can compete with them either in the extent and fertility of their own and the Messenian territory, or in the number of their slaves, and especially of the Helots, or of their horses, or of the animals which feed on the Messenian pastures. But I have said enough of this: and as to gold and silver, there is more of them in Lacedaemon than in all the rest of Hellas, for during many generations gold has been always flowing in to them from the whole Hellenic world, and often from the barbarian also, and never going out, as in the fable of Aesop the fox said to the lion, ‘The prints of the feet of those going in are distinct enough;’ but who ever saw the trace of money going out of Lacedaemon? And therefore you may safely infer that the inhabitants are the richest of the Hellenes in gold and silver, and that their kings are the richest of them, for they have a larger share of these things, and they have also a tribute paid to them which is very considerable. Yet the Spartan wealth, though great in comparison of the wealth of the other Hellenes, is as nothing in comparison of that of the Persians and their kings. Why, I have been informed by a credible person who went up to the king (at Susa), that he passed through a large tract of excellent land, extending for nearly a day’s journey, which the people of the country called the queen’s girdle, and another, which they called her veil; and several other fair and fertile districts, which were reserved for the adornment of the queen, and are named after her several habiliments. Now, I cannot help thinking to myself, What if some one were to go to Amestris, the wife of Xerxes and mother of Artaxerxes, and say to her, There is a certain Dinomache, whose whole wardrobe is not worth fifty minae — and that will be more than the value — and she has a son who is possessed of a three-hundred acre patch at Erchiae, and he has a mind to go to war with your son — would she not wonder to what this Alcibiades trusts for success in the conflict? ‘He must rely,’ she would say to herself, ‘upon his training and wisdom — these are the things which Hellenes value.’ And if she heard that this Alcibiades who is making the attempt is not as yet twenty years old, and is wholly uneducated, and when his lover tells him that he ought to get education and training first, and then go and fight the king, he refuses, and says that he is well enough as he is, would she not be amazed, and ask ‘On what, then, does the youth rely?’ And if we replied: He relies on his beauty, and stature, and birth, and mental endowments, she would think that we were mad, Alcibiades, when she compared the advantages which you possess with those of her own people. And I believe that even Lampido, the daughter of Leotychides, the wife of Archidamus and mother of Agis, all of whom were kings, would have the same feeling; if, in your present uneducated state, you were to turn your thoughts against her son, she too would be equally astonished. But how disgraceful, that we should not have as high a notion of what is required in us as our enemies’ wives and mothers have of the qualities which are required in their assailants! O my friend, be persuaded by me, and hear the Delphian inscription, ‘Know thyself’ — not the men whom you think, but these kings are our rivals, and we can only overcome them by pains and skill. And if you fail in the required qualities, you will fail also in becoming renowned among Hellenes and Barbarians, which you seem to desire more than any other man ever desired anything.","st":"121b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I entirely believe you; but what are the sort of pains which are required, Socrates, — can you tell me?","st":"121b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Yes, I can; but we must take counsel together concerning the manner in which both of us may be most improved. For what I am telling you of the necessity of education applies to myself as well as to you; and there is only one point in which I have an advantage over you.","st":"121b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> What is that?","st":"121b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I have a guardian who is better and wiser than your guardian, Pericles.","st":"121b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Who is he, Socrates?","st":"121b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> God, Alcibiades, who up to this day has not allowed me to converse with you; and he inspires in me the faith that I am especially designed to bring you to honour.","st":"121b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> You are jesting, Socrates.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Perhaps, at any rate, I am right in saying that all men greatly need pains and care, and you and I above all men.","st":"124c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> You are not far wrong about me.","st":"124c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And certainly not about myself.","st":"124c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> But what can we do?","st":"124c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> There must be no hesitation or cowardice, my friend.","st":"124c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That would not become us, Socrates.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> No, indeed, and we ought to take counsel together: for do we not wish to be as good as possible?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> We do.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> In what sort of virtue?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Plainly, in the virtue of good men.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Who are good in what?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Those, clearly, who are good in the management of affairs.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> What sort of affairs? Equestrian affairs?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> You mean that about them we should have recourse to horsemen?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Well, naval affairs?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> You mean that we should have recourse to sailors about them?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then what affairs? And who do them?","st":"124e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> The affairs which occupy Athenian gentlemen.","st":"124e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And when you speak of gentlemen, do you mean the wise or the unwise?","st":"124e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> The wise.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And a man is good in respect of that in which he is wise?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And evil in respect of that in which he is unwise?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> The shoemaker, for example, is wise in respect of the making of shoes?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then he is good in that?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> He is.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But in respect of the making of garments he is unwise?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then in that he is bad?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then upon this view of the matter the same man is good and also bad?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But would you say that the good are the same as the bad?","st":"125a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then whom do you call the good?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I mean by the good those who are able to rule in the city.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Not, surely, over horses?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But over men?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> When they are sick?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Or on a voyage?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Or reaping the harvest?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> When they are doing something or nothing?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> When they are doing something, I should say.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I wish that you would explain to me what this something is.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> When they are having dealings with one another, and using one another’s services, as we citizens do in our daily life.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Those of whom you speak are ruling over men who are using the services of other men?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Are they ruling over the signal-men who give the time to the rowers?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No; they are not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> That would be the office of the pilot?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But, perhaps you mean that they rule over flute-players, who lead the singers and use the services of the dancers?","st":"125d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"125d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> That would be the business of the teacher of the chorus?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then what is the meaning of being able to rule over men who use other men?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I mean that they rule over men who have common rights of citizenship, and dealings with one another.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And what sort of an art is this? Suppose that I ask you again, as I did just now, What art makes men know how to rule over their fellow-sailors, — how would you answer?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> The art of the pilot.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And, if I may recur to another old instance, what art enables them to rule over their fellow-singers?","st":"125e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> The art of the teacher of the chorus, which you were just now mentioning.","st":"125e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And what do you call the art of fellow-citizens?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I should say, good counsel, Socrates.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And is the art of the pilot evil counsel?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But good counsel?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes, that is what I should say, — good counsel, of which the aim is the preservation of the voyagers.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> True. And what is the aim of that other good counsel of which you speak?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> The aim is the better order and preservation of the city.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And what is that of which the absence or presence improves and preserves the order of the city? Suppose you were to ask me, what is that of which the presence or absence improves or preserves the order of the body? I should reply, the presence of health and the absence of disease. You would say the same?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And if you were to ask me the same question about the eyes, I should reply in the same way, ‘the presence of sight and the absence of blindness;’ or about the ears, I should reply, that they were improved and were in better case, when deafness was absent, and hearing was present in them.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And what would you say of a state? What is that by the presence or absence of which the state is improved and better managed and ordered?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I should say, Socrates: — the presence of friendship and the absence of hatred and division.","st":"126c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And do you mean by friendship agreement or disagreement?","st":"126c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Agreement.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> What art makes cities agree about numbers?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Arithmetic.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And private individuals?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> The same.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And what art makes each individual agree with himself?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> The same.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And what art makes each of us agree with himself about the comparative length of the span and of the cubit? Does not the art of measure?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Individuals are agreed with one another about this; and states, equally?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And the same holds of the balance?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But what is the other agreement of which you speak, and about what? what art can give that agreement? And does that which gives it to the state give it also to the individual, so as to make him consistent with himself and with another?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I should suppose so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But what is the nature of the agreement? — answer, and faint not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I mean to say that there should be such friendship and agreement as exists between an affectionate father and mother and their son, or between brothers, or between husband and wife.","st":"126e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But can a man, Alcibiades, agree with a woman about the spinning of wool, which she understands and he does not?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No, truly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Nor has he any need, for spinning is a female accomplishment.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And would a woman agree with a man about the science of arms, which she has never learned?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"127a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I suppose that the use of arms would be regarded by you as a male accomplishment?","st":"127a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> It would.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then, upon your view, women and men have two sorts of knowledge?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then in their knowledge there is no agreement of women and men?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> There is not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Nor can there be friendship, if friendship is agreement?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Plainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then women are not loved by men when they do their own work?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I suppose not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Nor men by women when they do their own work?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> No.","st":"127b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Nor are states well administered, when individuals do their own work?","st":"127b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I should rather think, Socrates, that the reverse is the truth. (Compare Republic.)","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> What! do you mean to say that states are well administered when friendship is absent, the presence of which, as we were saying, alone secures their good order?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> But I should say that there is friendship among them, for this very reason, that the two parties respectively do their own work.","st":"127c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> That was not what you were saying before; and what do you mean now by affirming that friendship exists when there is no agreement? How can there be agreement about matters which the one party knows, and of which the other is in ignorance?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Impossible.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And when individuals are doing their own work, are they doing what is just or unjust?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> What is just, certainly.","st":"127d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And when individuals do what is just in the state, is there no friendship among them?","st":"127d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I suppose that there must be, Socrates.","st":"127d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then what do you mean by this friendship or agreement about which we must be wise and discreet in order that we may be good men? I cannot make out where it exists or among whom; according to you, the same persons may sometimes have it, and sometimes not.","st":"127d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> But, indeed, Socrates, I do not know what I am saying; and I have long been, unconsciously to myself, in a most disgraceful state.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Nevertheless, cheer up; at fifty, if you had discovered your deficiency, you would have been too old, and the time for taking care of yourself would have passed away, but yours is just the age at which the discovery should be made.","st":"127e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> And what should he do, Socrates, who would make the discovery?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Answer questions, Alcibiades; and that is a process which, by the grace of God, if I may put any faith in my oracle, will be very improving to both of us.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> If I can be improved by answering, I will answer.","st":"128a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And first of all, that we may not peradventure be deceived by appearances, fancying, perhaps, that we are taking care of ourselves when we are not, what is the meaning of a man taking care of himself? and when does he take care? Does he take care of himself when he takes care of what belongs to him?","st":"128a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I should think so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> When does a man take care of his feet? Does he not take care of them when he takes care of that which belongs to his feet?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I do not understand.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Let me take the hand as an illustration; does not a ring belong to the finger, and to the finger only?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"128b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And the shoe in like manner to the foot?","st":"128b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"128b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And when we take care of our shoes, do we not take care of our feet?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I do not comprehend, Socrates.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But you would admit, Alcibiades, that to take proper care of a thing is a correct expression?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And taking proper care means improving?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And what is the art which improves our shoes?","st":"128c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Shoemaking.","st":"128c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then by shoemaking we take care of our shoes?","st":"128c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And do we by shoemaking take care of our feet, or by some other art which improves the feet?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> By some other art.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And the same art improves the feet which improves the rest of the body?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Which is gymnastic?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then by gymnastic we take care of our feet, and by shoemaking of that which belongs to our feet?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And by gymnastic we take care of our hands, and by the art of graving rings of that which belongs to our hands?","st":"128d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And by gymnastic we take care of the body, and by the art of weaving and the other arts we take care of the things of the body?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then the art which takes care of each thing is different from that which takes care of the belongings of each thing?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then in taking care of what belongs to you, you do not take care of yourself?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> For the art which takes care of our belongings appears not to be the same as that which takes care of ourselves?","st":"128e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly not.","st":"128e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And now let me ask you what is the art with which we take care of ourselves?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I cannot say.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> At any rate, thus much has been admitted, that the art is not one which makes any of our possessions, but which makes ourselves better?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But should we ever have known what art makes a shoe better, if we did not know a shoe?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Impossible.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Nor should we know what art makes a ring better, if we did not know a ring?","st":"129a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is true.","st":"129a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And can we ever know what art makes a man better, if we do not know what we are ourselves?","st":"129a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Impossible.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And is self-knowledge such an easy thing, and was he to be lightly esteemed who inscribed the text on the temple at Delphi? Or is self-knowledge a difficult thing, which few are able to attain?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> At times I fancy, Socrates, that anybody can know himself; at other times the task appears to be very difficult.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But whether easy or difficult, Alcibiades, still there is no other way; knowing what we are, we shall know how to take care of ourselves, and if we are ignorant we shall not know.","st":"129b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Well, then, let us see in what way the self-existent can be discovered by us; that will give us a chance of discovering our own existence, which otherwise we can never know.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> You say truly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Come, now, I beseech you, tell me with whom you are conversing? — with whom but with me?","st":"129c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> As I am, with you?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> That is to say, I, Socrates, am talking?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And Alcibiades is my hearer?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And I in talking use words?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And talking and using words have, I suppose, the same meaning?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> To be sure.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And the user is not the same as the thing which he uses?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> What do you mean?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I will explain; the shoemaker, for example, uses a square tool, and a circular tool, and other tools for cutting?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But the tool is not the same as the cutter and user of the tool?","st":"129d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Of course not.","st":"129d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And in the same way the instrument of the harper is to be distinguished from the harper himself?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> It is.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Now the question which I asked was whether you conceive the user to be always different from that which he uses?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I do.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then what shall we say of the shoemaker? Does he cut with his tools only or with his hands?","st":"129e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> With his hands as well.","st":"129e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> He uses his hands too?","st":"129e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And does he use his eyes in cutting leather?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> He does.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And we admit that the user is not the same with the things which he uses?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then the shoemaker and the harper are to be distinguished from the hands and feet which they use?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And does not a man use the whole body?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"130a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And that which uses is different from that which is used?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then a man is not the same as his own body?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is the inference.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> What is he, then?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I cannot say.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Nay, you can say that he is the user of the body.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And the user of the body is the soul?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes, the soul.","st":"130b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And the soul rules?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Let me make an assertion which will, I think, be universally admitted.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> What is it?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> That man is one of three things.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> What are they?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Soul, body, or both together forming a whole.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But did we not say that the actual ruling principle of the body is man?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes, we did.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And does the body rule over itself?","st":"130c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"130c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> It is subject, as we were saying?","st":"130c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"130c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then that is not the principle which we are seeking?","st":"130c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> It would seem not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But may we say that the union of the two rules over the body, and consequently that this is man?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very likely.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> The most unlikely of all things; for if one of the members is subject, the two united cannot possibly rule.","st":"130d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"130d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But since neither the body, nor the union of the two, is man, either man has no real existence, or the soul is man?","st":"130d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Just so.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Is anything more required to prove that the soul is man?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not; the proof is, I think, quite sufficient.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And if the proof, although not perfect, be sufficient, we shall be satisfied; — more precise proof will be supplied when we have discovered that which we were led to omit, from a fear that the enquiry would be too much protracted.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> What was that?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> What I meant, when I said that absolute existence must be first considered; but now, instead of absolute existence, we have been considering the nature of individual existence, and this may, perhaps, be sufficient; for surely there is nothing which may be called more properly ourselves than the soul?","st":"130e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> There is nothing.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then we may truly conceive that you and I are conversing with one another, soul to soul?","st":"131a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very true.","st":"131a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And that is just what I was saying before — that I, Socrates, am not arguing or talking with the face of Alcibiades, but with the real Alcibiades; or in other words, with his soul.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"131b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then he who bids a man know himself, would have him know his soul?","st":"131b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That appears to be true.","st":"131b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> He whose knowledge only extends to the body, knows the things of a man, and not the man himself?","st":"131b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is true.","st":"131b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then neither the physician regarded as a physician, nor the trainer regarded as a trainer, knows himself?","st":"131b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> He does not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> The husbandmen and the other craftsmen are very far from knowing themselves, for they would seem not even to know their own belongings? When regarded in relation to the arts which they practise they are even further removed from self-knowledge, for they only know the belongings of the body, which minister to the body.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then if temperance is the knowledge of self, in respect of his art none of them is temperate?","st":"131c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I agree.","st":"131c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And this is the reason why their arts are accounted vulgar, and are not such as a good man would practise?","st":"131c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Again, he who cherishes his body cherishes not himself, but what belongs to him?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But he who cherishes his money, cherishes neither himself nor his belongings, but is in a stage yet further removed from himself?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I agree.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then the money-maker has really ceased to be occupied with his own concerns?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And if any one has fallen in love with the person of Alcibiades, he loves not Alcibiades, but the belongings of Alcibiades?","st":"131d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But he who loves your soul is the true lover?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is the necessary inference.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> The lover of the body goes away when the flower of youth fades?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But he who loves the soul goes not away, as long as the soul follows after virtue?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"131e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And I am the lover who goes not away, but remains with you, when you are no longer young and the rest are gone?","st":"131e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes, Socrates; and therein you do well, and I hope that you will remain.","st":"131e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then you must try to look your best.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I will.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> The fact is, that there is only one lover of Alcibiades the son of Cleinias; there neither is nor ever has been seemingly any other; and he is his darling, — Socrates, the son of Sophroniscus and Phaenarete.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"132a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And did you not say, that if I had not spoken first, you were on the point of coming to me, and enquiring why I only remained?","st":"132a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is true.","st":"132a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> The reason was that I loved you for your own sake, whereas other men love what belongs to you; and your beauty, which is not you, is fading away, just as your true self is beginning to bloom. And I will never desert you, if you are not spoiled and deformed by the Athenian people; for the danger which I most fear is that you will become a lover of the people and will be spoiled by them. Many a noble Athenian has been ruined in this way. For the demus of the great-hearted Erechteus is of a fair countenance, but you should see him naked; wherefore observe the caution which I give you.","st":"132a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> What caution?","st":"132b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Practise yourself, sweet friend, in learning what you ought to know, before you enter on politics; and then you will have an antidote which will keep you out of harm’s way.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Good advice, Socrates, but I wish that you would explain to me in what way I am to take care of myself.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Have we not made an advance? for we are at any rate tolerably well agreed as to what we are, and there is no longer any danger, as we once feared, that we might be taking care not of ourselves, but of something which is not ourselves.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And the next step will be to take care of the soul, and look to that?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Leaving the care of our bodies and of our properties to others?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very good.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But how can we have a perfect knowledge of the things of the soul? — For if we know them, then I suppose we shall know ourselves. Can we really be ignorant of the excellent meaning of the Delphian inscription, of which we were just now speaking?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> What have you in your thoughts, Socrates?","st":"132d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> I will tell you what I suspect to be the meaning and lesson of that inscription. Let me take an illustration from sight, which I imagine to be the only one suitable to my purpose.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> What do you mean?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Consider; if some one were to say to the eye, ‘See thyself,’ as you might say to a man, ‘Know thyself,’ what is the nature and meaning of this precept? Would not his meaning be: — That the eye should look at that in which it would see itself?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly.","st":"132e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And what are the objects in looking at which we see ourselves?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly, Socrates, in looking at mirrors and the like.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Very true; and is there not something of the nature of a mirror in our own eyes?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"133a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Did you ever observe that the face of the person looking into the eye of another is reflected as in a mirror; and in the visual organ which is over against him, and which is called the pupil, there is a sort of image of the person looking?","st":"133a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is quite true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then the eye, looking at another eye, and at that in the eye which is most perfect, and which is the instrument of vision, will there see itself?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is evident.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But looking at anything else either in man or in the world, and not to what resembles this, it will not see itself?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then if the eye is to see itself, it must look at the eye, and at that part of the eye where sight which is the virtue of the eye resides?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"133c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And if the soul, my dear Alcibiades, is ever to know herself, must she not look at the soul; and especially at that part of the soul in which her virtue resides, and to any other which is like this?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I agree, Socrates.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And do we know of any part of our souls more divine than that which has to do with wisdom and knowledge?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> There is none.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then this is that part of the soul which resembles the divine; and he who looks at this and at the whole class of things divine, will be most likely to know himself?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And self-knowledge we agree to be wisdom?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But if we have no self-knowledge and no wisdom, can we ever know our own good and evil?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> How can we, Socrates?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> You mean, that if you did not know Alcibiades, there would be no possibility of your knowing that what belonged to Alcibiades was really his?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> It would be quite impossible.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Nor should we know that we were the persons to whom anything belonged, if we did not know ourselves?","st":"133d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> How could we?","st":"133d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And if we did not know our own belongings, neither should we know the belongings of our belongings?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then we were not altogether right in acknowledging just now that a man may know what belongs to him and yet not know himself; nay, rather he cannot even know the belongings of his belongings; for the discernment of the things of self, and of the things which belong to the things of self, appear all to be the business of the same man, and of the same art.","st":"133e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> So much may be supposed.","st":"133e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And he who knows not the things which belong to himself, will in like manner be ignorant of the things which belong to others?","st":"133e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very true.","st":"133e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And if he knows not the affairs of others, he will not know the affairs of states?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then such a man can never be a statesman?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> He cannot.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Nor an economist?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> He cannot.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> He will not know what he is doing?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> He will not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And will not he who is ignorant fall into error?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Assuredly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And if he falls into error will he not fail both in his public and private capacity?","st":"134a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes, indeed.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And failing, will he not be miserable?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And what will become of those for whom he is acting?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> They will be miserable also.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then he who is not wise and good cannot be happy?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> He cannot.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> The bad, then, are miserable?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes, very.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And if so, not he who has riches, but he who has wisdom, is delivered from his misery?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Cities, then, if they are to be happy, do not want walls, or triremes, or docks, or numbers, or size, Alcibiades, without virtue? (Compare Arist. Pol.)","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Indeed they do not.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And you must give the citizens virtue, if you mean to administer their affairs rightly or nobly?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"134c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But can a man give that which he has not?","st":"134c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Impossible.","st":"134c"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then you or any one who means to govern and superintend, not only himself and the things of himself, but the state and the things of the state, must in the first place acquire virtue.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> You have not therefore to obtain power or authority, in order to enable you to do what you wish for yourself and the state, but justice and wisdom.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Clearly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> You and the state, if you act wisely and justly, will act according to the will of God?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> As I was saying before, you will look only at what is bright and divine, and act with a view to them?","st":"134d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"134d"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> In that mirror you will see and know yourselves and your own good?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And so you will act rightly and well?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"134e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> In which case, I will be security for your happiness.","st":"134e"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I accept the security.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> But if you act unrighteously, your eye will turn to the dark and godless, and being in darkness and ignorance of yourselves, you will probably do deeds of darkness.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Very possibly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> For if a man, my dear Alcibiades, has the power to do what he likes, but has no understanding, what is likely to be the result, either to him as an individual or to the state — for example, if he be sick and is able to do what he likes, not having the mind of a physician — having moreover tyrannical power, and no one daring to reprove him, what will happen to him? Will he not be likely to have his constitution ruined?","st":"135a"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Or again, in a ship, if a man having the power to do what he likes, has no intelligence or skill in navigation, do you see what will happen to him and to his fellow-sailors?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes; I see that they will all perish.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And in like manner, in a state, and where there is any power and authority which is wanting in virtue, will not misfortune, in like manner, ensue?","st":"135b"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Not tyrannical power, then, my good Alcibiades, should be the aim either of individuals or states, if they would be happy, but virtue.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is true.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And before they have virtue, to be commanded by a superior is better for men as well as for children? (Compare Arist. Pol.)","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> That is evident.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And that which is better is also nobler?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And what is nobler is more becoming?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then to the bad man slavery is more becoming, because better?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> True.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> Then vice is only suited to a slave?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And virtue to a freeman?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And, O my friend, is not the condition of a slave to be avoided?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Certainly, Socrates.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And are you now conscious of your own state? And do you know whether you are a freeman or not?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I think that I am very conscious indeed of my own state.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And do you know how to escape out of a state which I do not even like to name to my beauty?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Yes, I do.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> How?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> By your help, Socrates.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> That is not well said, Alcibiades.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> What ought I to have said?","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> By the help of God.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> I agree; and I further say, that our relations are likely to be reversed. From this day forward, I must and will follow you as you have followed me; I will be the disciple, and you shall be my master.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> O that is rare! My love breeds another love: and so like the stork I shall be cherished by the bird whom I have hatched.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Alcibiades:<\/span> Strange, but true; and henceforward I shall begin to think about justice.","st":"?"},{"sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"speaker-label\">Socrates:<\/span> And I hope that you will persist; although I have fears, not because I doubt you; but I see the power of the state, which may be too much for both of us.","st":"?"}],[]);})();
